			"text": "The site picker answers one question about every site at once, without you having to select them one at a time: has its newest release finished publishing to every environment? If it has not, the site carries an amber dot - on its row in the dropdown, and on the closed picker when it is the site you are looking at. Typing * filters the list to sites carrying a dot. Anything you type after it still has to match as usual, so *sample means outstanding and matches \"sample\" - not either one on its own. The * only does this at the front of what you type; it is a mode switch, not a wildcard. The dots are filled in by a background sweep that runs shortly after the app starts and again every 20 minutes - about two and a half seconds and one call per site. It never blocks anything you are doing: sites light up as their answers arrive, and refreshing a site updates its own dot immediately from data that refresh already fetched. A site the sweep cannot reach keeps whatever dot it had rather than going dark, because a failed call is not evidence that a release finished. With no TFS token configured, no dots appear at all and * matches nothing. While a sweep is running, the small dot in the top-right of the header - beside LAST REFRESH … · N CALLS - turns grey and pulses. It sits solid green the rest of the time; hover it for when the last sweep ran and what it cost. The sweep also records a single site release sweep row on the Diagnostics card, with its total time and call count. Its individual calls are deliberately kept off that card: dozens of release reads would bury the handful a refresh makes. Some sites never get a dot in either direction. Those are catalog entries with no release definition in TFS at all - the same sites whose Release Pipeline card reports the definition as missing when you select them."
