			"text": "With a query open, the grid can be driven from the keyboard without reaching for the mouse. The keys match GitHub's, so they should already be in your fingers. They also work when focus has landed on the window's own toolbar rather than the page - after clicking Refresh, for instance - so you don't have to click back into the grid first. The exception is when you are typing in a box or a drop-down, where a letter means what it always means. Key Does j Move down one row, wrapping from the last row back to the first. With nothing selected yet, selects the first row. k Move up one row, wrapping from the first row round to the last. With nothing selected yet, selects the last row. o Open the selected row's work item detail - the same thing clicking its title does. Does nothing when no row is selected. c Open Add Comment against the selected row. r Open Return Task against the selected row. t Open Triage against the selected row. x Open Close Task with State set to Closed. Available on every work-item type, including from the row's right-click menu. Space Tick or untick the selected row's checkbox while a batch flow is open - see Return Tasks below. Stays the browser's page-scroll key at every other time. Esc Clear the selection. Only when no modal is open; while one is, Escape closes that instead. The selected row is marked with a violet bar down its left edge - the first row of the grid under View Query Results shows it. The first keypress scrolls the query results into view if they had been scrolled past, and the grid keeps the selected row on screen as you move. Only rows you can actually see are visited, so the tag chips and the quick filter box narrow what j and k walk through. Sorting keeps your selection on the row you picked - it just moves with it. Switching or refreshing a query clears the selection, as does narrowing the rows. o, c, r, t and x are shown in the row's right-click menu, so the menu is also where you find them. c, r and t keep working with a work item open: from the Work Item Detail window they act on the item you are reading, which is the same thing its three header buttons do. They stop there — once a comment modal is up, the letters are just letters again. x also opens Close Task for the item in the detail window. Like the other action keys, it works when focus is on the WPF toolbar and is ignored while typing or while another modal is open. That window adds three letters of its own, for the header buttons that only read: Key Does i Copy the work item ID - the same thing clicking #1234 in the header does. v View in TFS - opens the item in your browser. s Share - copies a link to the item. Each one presses the button itself, so the Copied confirmation appears on the button you would have clicked. They follow the same rule as c, r and t: not while you are typing in a field, and not once a comment modal has opened over the top. All six also work when focus is still on the window's own toolbar — opening an item with o leaves it there, so you never have to click into the item first. The keys are ignored while you are typing in a field and while any modal is open, so they never fire in the middle of a comment. Inside a comment modal there is one key of its own: Ctrl+Enter presses OK, from any field, including in the batch Return Task modal. It does nothing until you turn on Submit on Ctrl+Enter in Settings — off by default, because a comment box is somewhere you type freely and a chord that submits from inside one should be something you asked for."
